-- Lesson 1: Navigating the x-y Plane
'{"hook":"A pilot, a game designer, and a GPS satellite all think in coordinates. Understanding the full x-y plane — including negative values — is how we navigate the modern world.","inventor":{"name":"René Descartes","birth_year":1596,"death_year":1650,"nationality":"French","bio":"Descartes extended his coordinate system to include negative numbers, giving us four quadrants instead of just one. This let mathematicians describe positions in ALL directions from a central point."},"year_invented":1637,"story_paragraphs":["When Descartes first described coordinates, he mainly worked in the positive region — up and to the right. But extending the axes in both directions (positive and negative) created four quadrants, each with its own character.","Quadrant I (top-right): both x and y are positive. Quadrant II (top-left): x is negative, y positive. Quadrant III (bottom-left): both negative. Quadrant IV (bottom-right): x positive, y negative. This covers every possible position on a flat surface.","Navigation was one of the first practical applications. Sailors had used latitude (north-south) and longitude (east-west) for centuries — essentially a coordinate system on the sphere of the Earth. Descartes''s flat coordinates formalized the same idea mathematically.","Today, coordinate systems are everywhere: the pixels on your screen, the cells in a spreadsheet (A1 = column A, row 1), the tiles on a chess board, and the GPS coordinates that guide your car. All are descendants of Descartes''s insight."],"key_contributors":[{"name":"Leonhard Euler","contribution":"Standardized the notation of using x for horizontal and y for vertical axes"}],"real_world_applications":[{"name":"Chess Notation","icon":"♟️","description":"Every square on a chessboard is identified by a coordinate like e4 or d7"},{"name":"Spreadsheets","icon":"📋","description":"Cell A1 is the coordinate (column 1, row 1) — spreadsheets are grids"},{"name":"Radar Systems","icon":"📡","description":"Air traffic control uses coordinates to track every aircraft in the sky"},{"name":"Pixel Art","icon":"🎨","description":"Digital images are grids of pixels, each at an (x, y) position with a color value"}],"image":{"url":null,"alt":"The four quadrants of the Cartesian plane","attribution":"Wikimedia Commons, public domain","source_url":null}}'::jsonb)
